// Prisma schema for Doxa.
//
// Phase 0 established the toolchain only. Phase 1 adds the auth/tenancy
// foundation: Profile (mirrors the Supabase-managed auth.users row, no
// credentials duplicated), Organization, and Membership.
//
// Profile.id is NOT declared as a Prisma relation to auth.users, and the
// datasource does NOT list Supabase's "auth" schema. That was tried and
// reverted: once `auth` is a tracked schema, `prisma migrate dev`'s drift
// detection compares the ENTIRE real auth schema (dozens of Supabase-owned
// tables) against our migration history and offers to reset the database
// to "fix" the mismatch — which would drop Supabase's live auth data. Not
// safe against a real Supabase project. Instead, a hand-written, unmanaged
// trigger (see prisma/migrations/*_add_auth_profiles_organizations/
// migration.sql) auto-creates a Profile row when a Supabase auth user
// signs up; there is deliberately no DB-level FK/cascade back from
// profiles to auth.users; see docs/multi-tenancy.md for the trade-off.
//
// Phase 2 adds the core information architecture: Space, Board, ItemType,
// Status, Category, Tag and Item. ItemType and Status are deliberately
// plain data tables (per-organization rows), never a hard-coded enum —
// see "Item Types" in docs/architecture.md. They (and Category) use a
// nullable `archivedAt` to soft-archive without breaking historical Items
// that still reference them; Tag is a lighter-weight label and is hard
// deleted instead (ItemTag rows cascade, Item.categoryId is SET NULL).
// Board gets an explicit `status` enum (ACTIVE/ARCHIVED) rather than
// `archivedAt`, matching the "status" property named explicitly in the
// Phase 2 brief for boards.
//
// Item has a cascading FK to Organization (via organizationId) alongside
// restrict-by-default FKs to ItemType/Status (itemTypeId/statusId), which
// looks like a multi-path-cascade hazard on Organization deletion —
// verified empirically that Postgres resolves it correctly (it deletes in
// dependency order within the one cascading DELETE), so no special
// handling is needed here.
//
// Phase 3 adds the community interaction layer: Vote, Comment (with
// self-referencing `parentId` for one level of threaded replies),
// CommentMention, ItemFollower, ItemActivity and Notification. All of
// them cascade-delete through their `itemId` FK (Item -> these tables),
// same pattern as Phase 2's Item -> ItemTag. Their Profile-referencing
// FKs (authorId/userId/actorId/mentionedId) deliberately follow Item's
// existing `author` convention instead — no onDelete specified — since a
// Profile is only ever deleted directly in test teardown, always after
// the owning Organization (and therefore these rows) is already gone;
// see e2e/utils/test-users.ts.
//
// Votes are one evidence source, not the prioritisation mechanism itself
// — no ranking/weighting logic lives in the schema. Vote counts are
// computed on read (`_count` on the `votes` relation) rather than
// denormalized onto Item, so they can never drift out of sync.
//
// Phase 4 adds Doxa's main differentiation: opinions are evidence, not
// automatically decisions. Four additions:
//
// - `Priority` — organisation-controlled data, same "data, not code"
//   rationale as ItemType/Status (see "Item Types" in
//   docs/architecture.md), seeded with None/Low/Medium/High/Critical.
//   `sortOrder` doubles as the severity rank used to sort Items by
//   priority. `Item.priorityId` is required, exactly like
//   itemTypeId/statusId, defaulting a new Item to the org's `isDefault`
//   priority ("None") the same way getDefaultStatus already works.
// - `ScoreCriterion` / `ItemScore` — a flexible, organisation-configured
//   prioritisation model. Criteria (Customer impact, Business value,
//   Effort, ...) are per-organisation rows an org opts into, never a
//   fixed set; `ItemScore` is a sparse (itemId, criterionId) -> value
//   table, so an Item is never forced to be scored on every dimension.
//   `weight` feeds a weighted-average computed on read
//   (features/scoring/queries.ts), never denormalized onto Item — same
//   never-drifts rationale as vote counts.
// - `Decision` — a first-class, append-only record of what was decided
//   about an Item and why. Recording a new Decision never updates or
//   deletes a previous row: an Item's decision HISTORY is every Decision
//   row ordered by createdAt, and the CURRENT decision is simply the most
//   recent one (features/decisions/queries.ts). This is what "opinions
//   are evidence, not automatically decisions" resolves to structurally
//   — votes/comments/scores never write here directly, only an explicit
//   recordDecision call does, and nothing about this table is ever
//   mutated or deleted by application code.
// - `RoadmapStage` on Decision — where (if anywhere) an Item sits on the
//   basic Now/Next/Later roadmap view, set explicitly per Decision rather
//   than inferred from DecisionType, so "should this appear on the
//   roadmap" stays a deliberate organisation call, not a side effect.
//
// ActivityType gains PRIORITY_CHANGED and DECISION_RECORDED, logged the
// same way STATUS_CHANGED already is, so an Item's Activity tab stays the
// single place to see everything that happened to it.
//
// Phase 5 prepares Doxa to operate as a commercial multi-tenant SaaS
// while keeping a useful free tier. Five additions:
//
// - `Plan` — a GLOBAL (not per-organisation) row per commercial tier
//   (FREE/PRO/BUSINESS): numeric limits (null = unlimited) and boolean
//   feature flags. This is the "central entitlement/feature system" the
//   brief asks for — application code never hard-codes a limit or a
//   `role === X` style plan check; it calls
//   features/entitlements/queries.ts, which reads these rows. Exact
//   commercial limits are configurable here, seeded by
//   features/billing/defaults.ts, without an application code change.
// - `Customer` / `Subscription` — one of each per Organisation (the
//   billing entity is the Organisation, matching how membership/roles
//   already work — a team/workspace billing model, not per-user).
//   `Customer.stripeCustomerId` / `Subscription.stripeSubscriptionId`
//   are nullable: every organisation gets a Customer+Subscription row on
//   FREE the moment it's created (see organizations/actions.ts), whether
//   or not Stripe is ever configured for this environment — "retaining a
//   useful free tier" means billing being unconfigured must never be a
//   reason an organisation lacks a plan. `SubscriptionStatus`,
//   `BillingPeriod`, `cancelAtPeriodEnd`/`canceledAt` model exactly the
//   Stripe subscription lifecycle so a future webhook handler
//   (features/billing/webhook.ts) has a natural, already-correct shape
//   to write into — see docs/environment.md for how STRIPE_* env vars
//   stay optional in local dev.
// - `Organization` gains branding fields (`logoUrl`, `accentColor`) and
//   configurable per-organisation Item terminology
//   (`itemTerminologySingular`/`Plural`) — the "Terminology ... per
//   organisation is a later-phase concern" the Phase 0 comment promised.
//   Setting non-default branding is gated behind the `branding`
//   entitlement (features/organizations/actions.ts), not a DB
//   constraint — the column always accepts a value so an org that later
//   downgrades doesn't lose its existing branding data.
// - `AuditLog` — organisation-level, append-only, for significant
//   admin/security events (member removed, role changed, org/branding
//   updated, board created, plan changed, decision recorded, ...). Same
//   shape as `ItemActivity` (nullable `actorId`, `data` JSON payload)
//   but organisation-scoped rather than item-scoped, and deliberately
//   never carries secrets/tokens in `data` — see
//   docs/security-principles.md's "Auditability" section, which named
//   this phase explicitly.
//
// Usage enforcement (max members/boards/items) is done with live COUNT
// queries against the existing Membership/Board/Item tables, not a
// separate usage-ledger table — same "compute on read, never
// denormalize, can't drift" rationale as vote counts (see the Phase 3
// note above). Storage/API-access/integrations/analytics entitlements
// are modeled as Plan flags now so the system can control them, but
// nothing enforces them yet because the underlying features (file
// storage, a public API, third-party integrations, an analytics
// dashboard) don't exist yet — same "prepare the architecture, don't
// build the feature" pattern as Phase 3's Notification model before
// email delivery existed.

generator client {
  provider = "prisma-client"
  output   = "../src/generated/prisma"
}

datasource db {
  provider = "postgresql"
}

enum MembershipRole {
  OWNER
  ADMIN
  MEMBER
}

/// Mirrors the subset of a Supabase auth user relevant to the app. Row is
/// created automatically by a database trigger when a user signs up (see
/// migration SQL) — application code never inserts this directly. `id`
/// equals the corresponding `auth.users.id`, by convention rather than a
/// Prisma-managed foreign key (see the note at the top of this file).
model Profile {
  id          String   @id @db.Uuid
  displayName String
  username    String?  @unique
  avatarUrl   String?
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime @updatedAt

  memberships         Membership[]
  items               Item[]
  votes               Vote[]
  comments            Comment[]
  mentionedIn         CommentMention[]
  itemFollows         ItemFollower[]
  activities          ItemActivity[]
  notifications       Notification[]     @relation("NotificationRecipient")
  notificationsCaused Notification[]     @relation("NotificationActor")
  decisionsRecorded   Decision[]
  auditLogEntries     AuditLog[]
  participantBlocks   ParticipantBlock[] @relation("BlockedParticipant")
  participantsBlocked ParticipantBlock[] @relation("BlockedBy")

  @@map("profiles")
}

model Organization {
  id                      String   @id @default(cuid())
  name                    String
  slug                    String   @unique
  /// Branding — gated behind the `branding` Plan entitlement when set to
  /// anything non-default (features/organizations/actions.ts); the
  /// column itself has no such constraint, so a downgraded org keeps its
  /// existing data even though it can no longer change it.
  logoUrl                 String?
  accentColor             String?
  /// What this organisation calls an Item in its own UI (e.g. "Ticket" /
  /// "Tickets" instead of "Item" / "Items") — see the Phase 0 promise in
  /// this file's top-of-file comment. Applied in the highest-traffic
  /// item-related copy only (features/organizations/terminology.ts); not
  /// swept across every string in the app.
  itemTerminologySingular String   @default("Item")
  itemTerminologyPlural   String   @default("Items")
  createdAt               DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt               DateTime @updatedAt

  memberships       Membership[]
  spaces            Space[]
  boards            Board[]
  itemTypes         ItemType[]
  statuses          Status[]
  priorities        Priority[]
  categories        Category[]
  tags              Tag[]
  items             Item[]
  scoreCriteria     ScoreCriterion[]
  decisions         Decision[]
  notifications     Notification[]
  customer          Customer?
  subscription      Subscription?
  auditLogs         AuditLog[]
  participantBlocks ParticipantBlock[]

  @@map("organizations")
}

/// A user's role within one organisation. `(organizationId, userId)` is
/// unique — one membership per user per org.
model Membership {
  id             String         @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  userId         String         @db.Uuid
  role           MembershipRole @default(MEMBER)
  createdAt      DateTime       @default(now())

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  user         Profile      @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([organizationId, userId])
  @@map("memberships")
}

/// A person the organisation's team has barred from voting, commenting,
/// following and submitting on its public boards. Per organisation: being
/// blocked by one organisation says nothing about another. Members can't
/// be blocked this way — remove the membership instead.
model ParticipantBlock {
  id             String   @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  userId         String   @db.Uuid
  blockedById    String   @db.Uuid
  createdAt      DateTime @default(now())

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  user         Profile      @relation("BlockedParticipant", fields: [userId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  blockedBy    Profile      @relation("BlockedBy", fields: [blockedById], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([organizationId, userId])
  @@map("participant_blocks")
}

/// A logical grouping within an organisation (e.g. "Product", "Customer
/// Feedback"). Contains Boards. Soft-archived via `archivedAt` rather than
/// deleted, so archiving never orphans its Boards/Items.
model Space {
  id             String    @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  name           String
  slug           String
  description    String?
  archivedAt     DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime  @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  boards       Board[]
  items        Item[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("spaces")
}

enum BoardVisibility {
  PUBLIC
  PRIVATE
}

enum BoardStatus {
  ACTIVE
  ARCHIVED
}

/// Where Items are submitted. `visibility` gates whether an unauthenticated
/// visitor may view it at all (see the public `/b/[org]/[board]` routes);
/// `status` is the archive lifecycle, kept separate from visibility.
model Board {
  id              String          @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId  String
  spaceId         String
  name            String
  slug            String
  description     String?
  visibility      BoardVisibility @default(PRIVATE)
  status          BoardStatus     @default(ACTIVE)
  /// Whether an Item submitted by a community participant waits for a
  /// team member to approve it before it shows on the public board. On by
  /// default; team members' own submissions never wait.
  requireApproval Boolean         @default(true)
  /// Reserved for future per-board configuration (e.g. custom submission
  /// fields, enabled item types). Not yet read or written by application
  /// logic beyond the default empty object — see the Phase 2 brief's
  /// "configuration" board property.
  settings        Json            @default("{}")
  createdAt       DateTime        @default(now())
  updatedAt       DateTime        @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  space        Space        @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  items        Item[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("boards")
}

/// A configurable kind of Item (Feature, Bug, Idea, ...) — data, not an
/// application-level enum, so an organisation can add/rename/archive its
/// own types. See "Item Types" in docs/architecture.md.
model ItemType {
  id             String    @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  name           String
  slug           String
  description    String?
  sortOrder      Int       @default(0)
  archivedAt     DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime  @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  items        Item[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("item_types")
}

/// A configurable workflow status (Open, Planned, ...) — same "data, not
/// code" rationale as ItemType. `isDefault` marks the status a new Item
/// starts in.
model Status {
  id             String    @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  name           String
  slug           String
  color          String?
  sortOrder      Int       @default(0)
  isDefault      Boolean   @default(false)
  archivedAt     DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime  @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  items        Item[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("statuses")
}

/// A configurable priority level (None, Low, Medium, High, Critical by
/// default) — same "data, not code" rationale as ItemType/Status, so an
/// organisation can rename/reorder/archive its own levels rather than
/// being locked into a hard-coded scale. `sortOrder` doubles as the
/// severity rank (None < Low < ... < Critical) used to sort Items by
/// priority in the admin prioritisation view.
model Priority {
  id             String    @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  name           String
  slug           String
  color          String?
  sortOrder      Int       @default(0)
  isDefault      Boolean   @default(false)
  archivedAt     DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime  @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  items        Item[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("priorities")
}

/// A single classification an Item can belong to. Unlike ItemType/Status,
/// deleting one doesn't threaten historical data — Item.categoryId just
/// goes null — so this is a plain hard-delete CRUD, no archive state.
model Category {
  id             String   @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  name           String
  slug           String
  createdAt      DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  items        Item[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("categories")
}

/// A free-form label. Items can have multiple. Hard-delete, same rationale
/// as Category.
model Tag {
  id             String   @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  name           String
  slug           String
  createdAt      DateTime @default(now())

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  items        ItemTag[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("tags")
}

model ItemTag {
  itemId String
  tagId  String

  item Item @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  tag  Tag  @relation(fields: [tagId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@id([itemId, tagId])
  @@map("item_tags")
}

/// An organisation-configured prioritisation dimension (Customer impact,
/// Business value, Strategic alignment, Effort, Urgency, Confidence, or
/// anything else an org defines) an Item can optionally be scored
/// against. Never a fixed set of dimensions — an organisation with none
/// configured sees no scoring UI at all. `weight` feeds the
/// weighted-average score computed on read
/// (features/scoring/queries.ts#computeItemScore); like ItemType/Status,
/// this is archived rather than deleted so historical ItemScores keep
/// their meaning.
model ScoreCriterion {
  id             String    @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  name           String
  slug           String
  description    String?
  weight         Float     @default(1)
  sortOrder      Int       @default(0)
  archivedAt     DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime  @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  scores       ItemScore[]

  @@unique([organizationId, slug])
  @@map("score_criteria")
}

/// One Item's score (1-5) against one ScoreCriterion. `@@unique` gives
/// "at most one score per item per criterion" as a database guarantee,
/// the same upsert-a-row shape as Vote. This is the BUSINESS SIGNAL half
/// of an Item's evidence (see docs/architecture.md) — distinct from, and
/// never implied by, community signal (votes/comments/followers).
model ItemScore {
  id          String   @id @default(cuid())
  itemId      String
  criterionId String
  value       Int
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt   DateTime @updatedAt

  item      Item           @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  criterion ScoreCriterion @relation(fields: [criterionId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)

  @@unique([itemId, criterionId])
  @@map("item_scores")
}

/// The central object in Doxa — never hard-coded as "feature request"; its
/// shape (type) and workflow (status) are both organisation-configured
/// data. `archivedAt`/`deletedAt` are deliberately separate: archived
/// Items are hidden from default views but fully restorable by their
/// author or an org admin; `deletedAt` is reserved for a future
/// moderation/hard-delete feature and is never set by Phase 2 code — see
/// the "archived/deleted state" field in the Phase 2 brief.
/// Who created an Item. Only TEAM items count toward a plan's `maxItems` —
/// pricing is per team, so what the community submits never raises anyone's bill.
/// Every Item is TEAM until community submissions exist.
enum ItemOrigin {
  TEAM
  COMMUNITY
}

model Item {
  id             String     @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  spaceId        String
  boardId        String
  itemTypeId     String
  statusId       String
  priorityId     String
  categoryId     String?
  authorId       String     @db.Uuid
  origin         ItemOrigin @default(TEAM)
  /// A community submission the team hasn't approved yet: visible to the
  /// team and its author only, and left out of every count and roadmap.
  awaitingReview Boolean    @default(false)
  title          String
  slug           String
  description    String?
  archivedAt     DateTime?
  deletedAt      DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime   @default(now())
  updatedAt      DateTime   @updatedAt

  organization  Organization   @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  space         Space          @relation(fields: [spaceId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  board         Board          @relation(fields: [boardId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  itemType      ItemType       @relation(fields: [itemTypeId], references: [id])
  status        Status         @relation(fields: [statusId], references: [id])
  priority      Priority       @relation(fields: [priorityId], references: [id])
  category      Category?      @relation(fields: [categoryId], references: [id], onDelete: SetNull)
  author        Profile        @relation(fields: [authorId], references: [id])
  tags          ItemTag[]
  votes         Vote[]
  comments      Comment[]
  followers     ItemFollower[]
  activities    ItemActivity[]
  scores        ItemScore[]
  decisions     Decision[]
  notifications Notification[]

  @@unique([boardId, slug])
  @@map("items")
}

/// One user's vote on one Item — evidence of interest, never the final
/// prioritisation signal itself (see docs/architecture.md). The
/// `@@unique` pair is what makes "one active vote per user per Item" and
/// "prevent duplicate votes" a database guarantee rather than an
/// application-level race: two concurrent "add vote" requests both race
/// to insert the same (itemId, userId) row, and the loser gets a unique
/// constraint violation instead of a duplicate row — see
/// `features/votes/actions.ts`.
model Vote {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  itemId    String
  userId    String   @db.Uuid
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  item Item    @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  user Profile @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id])

  @@unique([itemId, userId])
  @@map("votes")
}

/// A comment on an Item. `parentId` gives one level of threaded replies
/// (a reply's own `parentId` is deliberately not validated against being
/// itself a reply — see `features/comments/schema.ts` — replies-to-replies
/// collapse to the top-level thread in the UI, keeping the discussion
/// system flat and simple per the Phase 3 brief). Deletion is always
/// soft (`deletedAt` + `body` cleared) so thread structure survives a
/// removed comment — the UI renders a "[deleted]" placeholder instead of
/// leaving a hole where a reply's parent used to be.
model Comment {
  id        String    @id @default(cuid())
  itemId    String
  authorId  String    @db.Uuid
  parentId  String?
  body      String
  editedAt  DateTime?
  deletedAt DateTime?
  createdAt DateTime  @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime  @updatedAt

  item          Item             @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  author        Profile          @relation(fields: [authorId], references: [id])
  parent        Comment?         @relation("CommentReplies", fields: [parentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  replies       Comment[]        @relation("CommentReplies")
  mentions      CommentMention[]
  notifications Notification[]

  @@index([itemId])
  @@map("comments")
}

/// A structured record of an `@mention` inside a Comment — parsed once at
/// write time (see `features/comments/mentions.ts`) and stored as a real
/// row, rather than re-parsing the comment body's `@username` text on
/// every read. Only usernames belonging to the Item's own organisation
/// resolve to a mention; anything else is left as plain text.
model CommentMention {
  id          String   @id @default(cuid())
  commentId   String
  mentionedId String   @db.Uuid
  createdAt   DateTime @default(now())

  comment   Comment @relation(fields: [commentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  mentioned Profile @relation(fields: [mentionedId], references: [id])

  @@unique([commentId, mentionedId])
  @@map("comment_mentions")
}

/// A user following an Item, to receive Notifications about it. Same
/// add/remove-a-row shape as Vote, including the uniqueness guarantee
/// against duplicate follow rows.
model ItemFollower {
  id        String   @id @default(cuid())
  itemId    String
  userId    String   @db.Uuid
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())

  item Item    @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  user Profile @relation(fields: [userId], references: [id])

  @@unique([itemId, userId])
  @@map("item_followers")
}

enum ActivityType {
  ITEM_CREATED
  ITEM_EDITED
  STATUS_CHANGED
  PRIORITY_CHANGED
  VOTE_ADDED
  VOTE_REMOVED
  COMMENT_ADDED
  ITEM_ARCHIVED
  ITEM_RESTORED
  DECISION_RECORDED
}

/// An append-only history entry for one Item. Written alongside the
/// triggering mutation (same transaction where practical — see
/// `features/activity/log.ts`), never reconstructed after the fact from
/// other tables. `data` carries just enough type-specific context to
/// render the entry (e.g. `{ fromStatus, toStatus }` for
/// STATUS_CHANGED) without a further join. Deliberately excludes
/// low-signal UI events (opening the item, filtering a board) — see
/// "Do not record meaningless UI events" in the Phase 3 brief.
model ItemActivity {
  id        String       @id @default(cuid())
  itemId    String
  actorId   String?      @db.Uuid
  type      ActivityType
  data      Json         @default("{}")
  createdAt DateTime     @default(now())

  item  Item     @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  actor Profile? @relation(fields: [actorId], references: [id])

  @@index([itemId])
  @@map("item_activities")
}

enum DecisionType {
  PLANNED
  IN_PROGRESS
  COMPLETED
  DECLINED
  DEFERRED
  DUPLICATE
}

/// Where (if anywhere) a Decision currently places its Item on the basic
/// Now/Next/Later roadmap view — set explicitly per Decision rather than
/// inferred from DecisionType, so "should this appear on the roadmap, and
/// where" stays a deliberate organisation call, not a side effect of
/// recording a decision.
enum RoadmapStage {
  NOW
  NEXT
  LATER
}

/// A first-class, immutable record of what was decided about an Item and
/// why. Decisions are append-only: recording a new one never updates or
/// deletes a previous row, so an Item's full decision HISTORY is every
/// Decision row for it ordered by createdAt, and the CURRENT decision is
/// simply the most recent one (see features/decisions/queries.ts — there
/// is deliberately no update/delete Server Action for this model). This
/// is what "opinions are evidence, not automatically decisions" resolves
/// to structurally: votes, comments, and scores never write here
/// directly, only an explicit recordDecision call does. `rationale` is
/// required — a Decision without a stated reason defeats the point of
/// this entity. `internalNotes` is never shown on the public Item page,
/// unlike `type`/`rationale`/`targetDate`, which communicate the decision
/// back to the community that raised it.
model Decision {
  id             String        @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  itemId         String
  type           DecisionType
  rationale      String
  targetDate     DateTime?
  internalNotes  String?
  roadmapStage   RoadmapStage?
  createdById    String        @db.Uuid
  createdAt      DateTime      @default(now())

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  item         Item         @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  createdBy    Profile      @relation(fields: [createdById], references: [id])

  @@index([itemId, createdAt])
  @@map("decisions")
}

enum NotificationType {
  ITEM_COMMENT
  COMMENT_REPLY
  ITEM_STATUS_CHANGED
  /// A decision was recorded on an Item the recipient voted on or follows.
  ITEM_DECISION
  /// A community participant submitted an Item (sent to the team).
  ITEM_SUBMITTED
  MENTION
}

/// An in-app notification for one recipient. Deliberately carries
/// `organizationId` directly (not just derived via `item`/`comment`) so a
/// recipient's notification centre — and every read of this table — stays
/// a single tenant-scoped query, matching the rule in
/// docs/multi-tenancy.md. `readAt` is the only mutable field after
/// creation. No email delivery in Phase 3: the shape here (recipient,
/// type, actor, and the item/comment it's about) is exactly what a future
/// email job would need to consume, so that's additive later, not a
/// rework of this model.
model Notification {
  id             String           @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  userId         String           @db.Uuid
  type           NotificationType
  itemId         String?
  commentId      String?
  actorId        String?          @db.Uuid
  data           Json             @default("{}")
  readAt         DateTime?
  createdAt      DateTime         @default(now())

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  user         Profile      @relation("NotificationRecipient", fields: [userId], references: [id])
  item         Item?        @relation(fields: [itemId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  comment      Comment?     @relation(fields: [commentId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  actor        Profile?     @relation("NotificationActor", fields: [actorId], references: [id])

  @@index([userId, readAt])
  @@map("notifications")
}

enum PlanKey {
  FREE
  PRO
  BUSINESS
}

/// A commercial plan tier — GLOBAL data (three rows total, one per
/// `PlanKey`), not per-organisation, unlike Status/Priority/ItemType.
/// Every FREE-tier organisation shares this one row and its limits.
/// `null` on any `max*` column means unlimited. This is the single
/// source of truth entitlements are resolved from
/// (features/entitlements/queries.ts) — never a hard-coded number or a
/// `plan === "PRO"` check scattered through feature code.
model Plan {
  id          String  @id @default(cuid())
  key         PlanKey @unique
  name        String
  description String?

  /// Evaluated per owning user across every organisation they own, not
  /// per-organisation — see features/entitlements/queries.ts.
  maxOrganizations Int?
  maxMembers       Int?
  maxBoards        Int?
  maxItems         Int?
  /// Not yet enforced anywhere — there is no file-storage feature yet
  /// (Attachments, per docs/architecture.md, is still unbuilt).
  maxStorageMb     Int?

  /// Gates /org/[slug]/prioritization (features/prioritization) — basic
  /// per-item priority/scoring/decisions stay free on every plan; this
  /// is specifically the cross-board admin rollup view.
  advancedPrioritisation Boolean @default(false)
  /// Not yet enforced — no analytics dashboard feature exists yet.
  analytics              Boolean @default(false)
  /// Gates setting a non-default logoUrl/accentColor
  /// (features/organizations/actions.ts#updateOrganizationBranding).
  branding               Boolean @default(false)
  /// Not yet enforced — no public API exists yet.
  apiAccess              Boolean @default(false)
  /// Not yet enforced — no third-party integrations exist yet.
  integrations           Boolean @default(false)

  priceMonthlyCents    Int?
  priceYearlyCents     Int?
  stripePriceIdMonthly String?
  stripePriceIdYearly  String?

  sortOrder Int      @default(0)
  createdAt DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt DateTime @updatedAt

  subscriptions Subscription[]

  @@map("plans")
}

/// The billing identity for one Organisation — one-to-one, created
/// alongside every Organisation (whether or not Stripe is configured for
/// this environment) so "does this org have a Customer record" is never
/// a question application code has to handle as a special case.
/// `stripeCustomerId` stays null until a real Stripe Customer is created
/// for it (features/billing/actions.ts), which only happens once Stripe
/// env vars are present.
model Customer {
  id               String   @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId   String   @unique
  stripeCustomerId String?  @unique
  billingEmail     String?
  createdAt        DateTime @default(now())
  updatedAt        DateTime @updatedAt

  organization Organization  @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  subscription Subscription?

  @@map("customers")
}

enum SubscriptionStatus {
  ACTIVE
  TRIALING
  PAST_DUE
  CANCELED
  INCOMPLETE
}

enum BillingPeriod {
  MONTHLY
  YEARLY
}

/// One Organisation's current plan. Modeled after Stripe's own
/// Subscription object on purpose (status/billingPeriod/period bounds/
/// cancelAtPeriodEnd/canceledAt) so a future webhook handler has a
/// natural, already-correct shape to write into — see the top-of-file
/// Phase 5 note. Every Organisation has exactly one Subscription row,
/// starting on FREE at creation; a "cancellation" is a status/flag
/// transition on this same row, never a delete — matching how Stripe
/// itself represents cancellation (the subscription object persists
/// through `canceled`).
model Subscription {
  id                   String             @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId       String             @unique
  customerId           String             @unique
  planId               String
  status               SubscriptionStatus @default(ACTIVE)
  billingPeriod        BillingPeriod      @default(MONTHLY)
  stripeSubscriptionId String?            @unique
  currentPeriodStart   DateTime?
  currentPeriodEnd     DateTime?
  cancelAtPeriodEnd    Boolean            @default(false)
  canceledAt           DateTime?
  createdAt            DateTime           @default(now())
  updatedAt            DateTime           @updatedAt

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  customer     Customer     @relation(fields: [customerId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  plan         Plan         @relation(fields: [planId], references: [id])

  @@map("subscriptions")
}

enum AuditLogAction {
  MEMBER_ADDED
  MEMBER_REMOVED
  MEMBER_ROLE_CHANGED
  ORGANIZATION_UPDATED
  BRANDING_UPDATED
  BOARD_CREATED
  BOARD_ARCHIVED
  BOARD_RESTORED
  DECISION_RECORDED
  PLAN_CHANGED
}

/// An append-only, organisation-scoped record of significant
/// administrative/security events — never mutated or deleted by
/// application code, same append-only shape as ItemActivity/Decision.
/// `data` carries only non-secret, descriptive context (e.g.
/// `{ fromRole, toRole }`) — never a token, key, or other credential;
/// see docs/security-principles.md's "Auditability" section.
model AuditLog {
  id             String         @id @default(cuid())
  organizationId String
  actorId        String?        @db.Uuid
  action         AuditLogAction
  targetType     String?
  targetId       String?
  data           Json           @default("{}")
  createdAt      DateTime       @default(now())

  organization Organization @relation(fields: [organizationId], references: [id], onDelete: Cascade)
  actor        Profile?     @relation(fields: [actorId], references: [id])

  @@index([organizationId, createdAt])
  @@map("audit_logs")
}
